issues.problematic_code_snippet:
static async hasPendingInviteByUserId({ userId }: { userId: number }): Promise<boolean> {
    const pendingInvite = await prisma.membership.findFirst({
      where: {
        userId,
        accepted: true,
      },
      select: {
        id: true,
      },
    });
    return !!pendingInvite;
  }

// Determine which onboarding path to use
  const onboardingV3Enabled = await featuresRepository.checkIfFeatureIsEnabledGlobally("onboarding-v3");

  const hasPendingInvite = await MembershipRepository.hasPendingInviteByUserId({ userId });

  if (hasPendingInvite || onboardingV3Enabled) {
    return "/onboarding/personal/settings";
  }

  return onboardingV3Enabled ? "/onboarding/getting-started" : "/getting-started";

findings.body:
`hasPendingInviteByUserId` queries `accepted: true`. The method name, the JSDoc-style intent in the PR, and the integration tests all expect `accepted: false`. As written, any user with at least one accepted team membership returns `true`.

The effect is that a regular team member who opens `/onboarding/getting-started` is redirected to `/onboarding/personal/settings`, so the plan-selection page is unreachable for them. The same wrong boolean also drives `checkOnboardingRedirect`, so the root page and event-types page route any team member to personal onboarding.

::diff{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.ts" lines="584-596"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The human checklist asks to verify that `accepted: false` identifies invited users. In this code the answer is the opposite: the query selects accepted memberships.

::callout{type="info"}
If the intent is 'any unaccepted invite', `accepted: false` is the correct filter. Note that it also matches users who were invited to a team they never joined, which is part of the edge case in the checklist.

The original condition was `pendingInvite && onboardingV3Enabled`, which only redirected users with an invite when v3 was on. The PR changes it to `hasPendingInvite || onboardingV3Enabled`. The `||` means that whenever `onboarding-v3` is on, every user who reaches this function is sent to `/onboarding/personal/settings`, including users who never had an invite. That bypasses the `/onboarding/getting-started` flow that the v3 rollout is designed to show.

The final ternary on the next line, `onboardingV3Enabled ? "/onboarding/getting-started" : "/getting-started"`, is now effectively dead for the v3-on case. Reaching it requires `onboardingV3Enabled` to be false, so the first branch can never be taken.

The change is also not described in the PR, which is about the getting-started page only. It affects the root page (`apps/web/app/page.tsx`) and the event-types page.

::diff{file="packages/features/auth/lib/onboardingUtils.ts" lines="64-73"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/auth/lib/onboardingUtils.ts" lines="69-69"}
  if (hasPendingInvite && onboardingV3Enabled) {

`hasPendingInviteByUserId` is a `static` method that calls the module-level `prisma` import. Every neighbouring instance method in this class (for example `hasAcceptedMembershipByEmail` a few lines above) uses `this.prismaClient`. This keeps the method out of the class's dependency-injection pattern, so tests cannot pass in a transaction or a mock client. It also means the name `hasPendingInvite` and the query semantics can drift apart without the type system noticing.

::diff{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.ts" lines="584-596"}

If the method must stay static because callers use `MembershipRepository.hasPendingInviteByUserId` directly, accept an optional `PrismaClient` argument so callers can inject one.
